\section{Results and Empirical Observations}
\label{sec:results}

The representative traces below are included to make the three-dimensional
behavior visible before the aggregate statistics.  They are not independent
experiments: each is one deterministic realization from the same simulation
family used by the paired study.  Statistical claims are taken from the
versioned ten-seed evidence that follows.

\subsection{Representative three-dimensional reconstruction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \IncludePGF{w3d_ou3_jonswap_medium_xykin.pgf}
  \small (a) Horizontal surge/sway kinematics.
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \IncludePGF{w3d_ou3_jonswap_medium_zkin.pgf}
  \small (b) Vertical kinematics.
\end{minipage}
\caption{Representative $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ JONSWAP reconstruction.  Horizontal
and vertical channels are shown together because the estimator is evaluated as
a 3-D motion estimator rather than as a heave-only filter.}
\label{fig:representative-3d-kinematics}
\end{figure*}

Horizontal error has a different physical source mix from vertical error.  To
first order a small attitude error leaks gravity into horizontal acceleration,
whereas the vertical channel has no corresponding first-order $g\,\delta\theta$
term.  The reduced analysis in Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits} therefore
predicts that improving horizontal performance is primarily an observability and
low-frequency acceleration-error problem, not simply a reason to tighten the
horizontal $S=0$ regularizer.  The full-filter evidence below is consistent with
that interpretation: the current isotropic regularizer performs well across the
tested headings, but no universal separate $x/y$ optimum is inferred from this
limited directional set.

\subsection{Attitude and adaptation behavior}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \IncludePGF{w3d_ou3_jonswap_medium.pgf}
  \small (a) Roll, pitch, and yaw reconstruction/error.
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \IncludePGF{w3d_ou3_jonswap_medium_tuner.pgf}
  \small (b) Wave-band tuning and applied parameters.
\end{minipage}
\caption{Representative attitude and adaptation traces.  The first tuner panel
shows the wave-band frequency implied by the applied OU time scale,
$f_{\mathrm{wave,appl}}=1/(2\tau_{\mathrm{appl}})$, which is the smoothed
schedule derived from the wave-period estimator.  The acceleration-band carrier
retained for wave-direction demodulation is intentionally omitted because it is
not used by the deployed OU-III adaptation path.}
\label{fig:attitude-and-tuning}
\end{figure*}

The trace is best read together with Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}: wave period and
band-limited acceleration variance are estimated from the measurement-only
proxy, converted to targets, and then smoothed before being applied.  The plot
therefore shows the parameters actually seen by the MEKF rather than an
obsolete carrier-frequency diagnostic from a different subsystem.

